@legoabram - I claimed my version is S2B1v4 because that's what the W25Q128JV reports in the BFP Table, so I had assumed that what the manual stated was indeed v4 (I now see that's definitely not consistent with JEDEC's spec)

Reading the datasheet for the gd25q16e, It actually looks like that might be S2B1v1? It claims that setting CS high after only writing 8 bits for opcode 0x01h will clear QE

I've requested a JEDEC account, and will attempt to update PR to attempt to support at least v5 / v1 (used by the gd25q16e) and v6 used by my W25Q128JV.

@legoabram Yeah, I was surprised too either:
A) The chip is actually pretty forgiving or something just happened to work
B) I opened a fresh chip I had lying around to test this, its possible the QE bit happened to be set on this one. The last freshly opened one did not have it set, so I assumed new ones would not, but I guess that was a bad thing to assume when reporting that something was working, so I apologize for that.

@zackcornelius  I am going to try this latest patch, force the qe bit off, make sure working with the chip fails, and then test the chip again to see if it works. I'll let you know how it goes.

I've updated this code with (hopeful) support for QER modes 1, 4, 5, 6, based on some updates from @natelust 

@legoabram Can you review and see if what I ended up with seems to match the spec. I couldn't find a good reference for QER modes 1 and 4 as to how to read SR2, so I'm hoping this is right.

@natelust Can you test this updated code with your adafruit board? I'm not sure if v1 or v5 is correct for your board (I think it's 1, given that the datasheet says QER gets cleared on a write to just SR1)

@nvlsianpu I think this is ready for review again

@nvlsianpu Yes, this does work for me with my board. I did notice a bug for the code path for S2B1v1, as noted in a comment. It seems to just be a typo.

@zackcornelius Im not 100% positive but I think it is v5, that is what I have been testing, and that is what seems to work. I think v1 reverses byte order, I am going to go read the code 1 more time.

Edit:
Yeah, page 13 of the chip manual seems to indicate the register bytes need to be sent in the order they are sent in the v5 code path, which is consistent with it working, but I am NOT an expert on this, so people who are might notice something I dont'

@zackcornelius My one hesitation is that the linux driver I linked makes reference to v1 and v4 not supporting the 35h command. (Which could make sense according to the wording of the QER bit documentation) But given that your chip explicitly declares itself as v4 in the BFP, but still supports the 35h command, I'm honestly not certain why that would be the case. To be certain, we'd almost have to cross-reference a lot of different chips with their commands, quirks, and the QER code they report... Which sounds like oodles of fun.

The big issue I see is that these docs typically don't include their BFP values, which makes it difficult to verify without buying the actual chips. Additionally, the lack of clear documentation of which mode a flash supports in the datasheet means that we will need to provide good documentation for what each mode does so that a user can reference their datasheet and determine which mode should work for their chip. Though, this should probably be done in a different pull request.

Otherwise, everything appears correct given what we know.

@natelust Seriously good catch on the QE bit already being set. That would have been rather confusing!
